WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:06.250
Din Djarin, The Mandalorian's titular bounty
hunter, has had a wild ride since he crossed

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.170
paths with the Child, also known as Grogu.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:14.190
Luckily, Djarin has a variety of weapons to
protect himself and his little companion.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.740
Let's take a look at some of Djarin's gear
but be warned, spoilers are ahead.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.789
Mandalorian helmets have long been associated
with the Star Wars creed, and Djarin's helmet

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:25.619
is no exception.

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:30.839
Just like with any other Mandalorian helmet,
Djarin's features a T-shaped visor and entirely

00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:32.369
conceals his face.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:36.800
Also, Djarin's tribe follows the Way of the
Mandalore, a religion where members are not

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.430
allowed to remove their helmets in public.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.789
If they do, they can never put them back on
again.

00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:42.920
"Why," you ask?

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.350
"This is the way."

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.530
"This is the way."

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:52.879
Djarin has been seen without his helmet in
public only twice so far; in "Chapter 8: Redemption,"

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:57.350
because he was badly injured and needed a
droid to tend his wounds, and in "Chapter

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.120
15: The Believer", to bypass an Imperial security
scan.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.790
Mando's helmet also comes with a variety of
features, such as tracking heat signatures,

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:09.790
long-range audio, and detecting recent footsteps
left on the ground.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:13.680
These perks are especially useful when Djarin
needs to locate nearby individuals, whether

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.420
they be enemies or those in need of assistance.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:22.120
Djarin's Mandalorian armor not only looks
cool, but it is also extremely durable.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:26.920
It's made out of a powerful metal alloy called
beskar, which is known to withstand blaster

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.780
fire and even the might of a lightsaber.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:33.950
Since Djarin's armor is badly damaged in the
show's sophomore episode, he brings a large

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:39.250
payment of beskar to his tribe's Armorer,
who uses it to craft a new cuirass.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:43.340
Instead of having a red-and-gray color scheme
in the vein of his previous armor, this shiny

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.310
new armor is metallic silver, just like his
helmet.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:51.170
In "Redemption," the Armorer announces the
formation of Djarin's clan, Clan Mudhorn,

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.880
which is made up of himself and the Child.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.160
To coincide with this formation, the Armorer
inscribes the skull of the mudhorn on his

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.210
right pauldron as his signet.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:04.060
Djarin's dual flamethrowers have come in handy
time and time again.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.420
For instance, in "Chapter 3: The Sin," Djarin
uses a flamethrower to torch an attacking

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:09.420
stormtrooper.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.210
He's even been known to bring the heat when
words fail him.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.890
"You understand this?"

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:18.530
"Whoa, easy."

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.230
Like any weapon in Star Wars, however, flamethrowers
have their limits.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.560
After Djarin is surrounded by bounty hunters
who all want the Child, he uses a flamethrower

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.890
to drive them away, but it runs out of juice
when he needs it most.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.200
While Boba Fett first used a variation of
this weapon in the animated segment from the

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.280
infamous Star Wars Holiday Special, the Amban
phase-pulse blaster in The Mandalorian seems

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.280
to be much more powerful.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:46.290
Throughout the course of the series, Djarin
uses this sniper rifle to either incapacitate

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.460
or disintegrate anyone who stands in his path.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:54.360
These enemies can be as big as a Trandoshan
or as small as a Jawa, both of whom were killed

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.980
in the same episode.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.930
Djarin also uses the blaster to tase a wide
variety of intergalactic enemies and creatures,

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.520
such as stormtroopers and even a krayt dragon.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:08.590
When Djarin returns to the Mandalorian hideout
with his beskar payment, the Armorer uses

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:13.100
the excess material to build him a special
weapon: whistling birds.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:16.270
These small guided munitions are placed in
his left vambrace.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:19.850
When deployed, the projectiles fly through
the air while making a whistling sound before

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:23.060
striking their targets and detonating a small
explosive.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:28.400
The Armorer tells Djarin that these projectiles
are rare, so he saves them for desperate moments.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.430
Star Wars fans have often associated jetpacks
with Mandalorian culture, yet Djarin didn't

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:37.520
receive his own until the final episode of
season 1.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.730
In "Redemption," the Armorer asks Mando if
he has ever trained in the "Rising Phoenix,"

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.209
to which he promptly responds that he did
when was a boy.

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:49.310
The Armorer then gives Mando a jetpack and
sends him on his way to reunite the Child

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:50.790
with its own kind.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.920
The jetpack comes in handy later in the episode
when Djarin uses it to attack a TIE fighter

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.500
that's piloted by Moff Gideon.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.550
Djarin also uses the jetpack in the subsequent
episode when he flies out of the belly of

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.220
an angry krayt dragon.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:07.280
However, despite all of its advantages in
warfare, the best part about the jetpack is

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.740
watching the Child's ears flop around when
Mando takes him for a ride.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.941
Flash charges aren't exactly commonplace in
the Star Wars universe, but for Djarin, they

00:04:15.941 --> 00:04:19.489
are especially useful in "Chapter 5: The Gunslinger."

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:24.629
This episode sees Mando working with aspiring
bounty hunter Toro Calican to capture a wanted

00:04:24.629 --> 00:04:26.669
assassin named Fennec Shand.

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:31.759
They soon discover that Shand is using a faraway
ridge as a makeshift sniper nest.

00:04:31.759 --> 00:04:35.909
After nightfall, Djarin and Calican use flash
charges to blind Shand so they can apprehend

00:04:35.909 --> 00:04:36.909
her.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.449
Before long, Calican decides to betray Mando.

00:04:39.449 --> 00:04:42.659
Djarin blinds Calican with a flash charge
and takes him out.

00:04:42.659 --> 00:04:44.569
It's not a big loss for the galaxy, though.

00:04:44.569 --> 00:04:48.770
In fact, Motto could probably sum up Calican
in two words.

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.470
"Womp rat."

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.870
Djarin's whipcord has helped him get out of
many deadly situations.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:59.050
In "Chapter 6: The Prisoner," for example,
Djarin takes on several New Republic security

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.199
droids.

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:04.229
Throughout the fight, Mando uses the grappling
hook to behead one droid and uses it again

00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:07.990
to bring another one closer to a droid that's
being incinerated.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.259
Djarin later finds himself locked in a jail
cell.

00:05:10.259 --> 00:05:15.039
Luckily, he escapes after he uses his whipcord
to tether a security droid near his cell door.

00:05:15.039 --> 00:05:18.589
He then rips off one of its arms, which he
uses to open the lock.

00:05:19.589 --> 00:05:21.849
Grenades are probably one of the most important
weapons for Djarin.

00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:25.990
In fact, we've seen him reach for them in
wildly different circumstances.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.920
One instance where they were especially useful
happens in "Chapter 11: The Heiress" when

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:34.810
Djarin, Bo-Katan Kryze, and two other Mandalorians
infiltrate an Imperial cruiser transporting

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:36.250
valuable weapons.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:40.080
After flying to the ship, the four warriors
try to take the ship's bridge but end up encountering

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.210
a squad of stormtroopers armed with heavy
repeating blasters, all while the captain

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:47.780
is attempting to crash the ship at the behest
of Moff Gideon.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.009
Djarin quickly devises a plan to get himself
and the others to the bridge.

00:05:51.009 --> 00:05:56.169
He asks the three Mandalorians to cover him
as he charges toward the remaining stormtroopers.

00:05:56.169 --> 00:05:59.210
After getting close enough, Djarin throws
two grenades at the enemies, blowing them

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.969
up, and creating a path to victory.

00:06:01.969 --> 00:06:05.330
When speaking of self-preservation, a wise
man once said…

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:09.550
"Hokey religions and ancient weapons are no
match for a good blaster at your side."

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:13.999
Even though Djarin knows next to nothing about
the so-called "hokey religion," he almost

00:06:13.999 --> 00:06:16.250
always has a blaster at his side.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:20.401
As he demonstrates many times throughout the
show, Djarin is very proficient with his blaster

00:06:20.401 --> 00:06:21.401
pistol.

00:06:21.401 --> 00:06:25.349
In fact, the initial fight scene that kicks
off the entire series features Mando using

00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:29.430
his pistol to shoot the control panel of a
door, which then slices a Quarren trawler

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:30.499
in half.

00:06:30.499 --> 00:06:34.500
And that's just the first of many memorable
scenes of Djarin relying on his pistol to

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.909
save the day and the Child.

